  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nHave you ever had someone that overstayed their welcome, be it at a \nparty or staying at your house? Well, the weather is doing that \nagain. Our friend the monsoon (which those of you who read my \ndiscussions know I love so much, note sarcasm) has returned and\nwill be around for a few more days.\n\nWe do have some shower and isolated thunderstorms around as I write \nthis, but nothing strong and the storms are moving. Monsoonal \nmoisture will continue to push northward today and increase the \ncoverage of showers and storms across the area. Coverage still looks \nscattered at best though (at most a 1 out of 3 chance). There is \ndecent model consensus in keeping a vast majority of the \nshowers and storms along and west of the Continental Divide and \nalso near Casper. We can't rule a shower or storm out anywhere, \nbut the chance is only around 1 in 10 or less. As for potential \nflooding, probably not through Wednesday night. The core of the \ndeepest moisture should remain south of Wyoming today. There is \nalso some steering flow, about 15 to 20 knots worth, which \nshould keep the showers and storms of the moving and not of the \nstationary variety. As for temperatures, this looks to be the \nwarmest day of the week with flow turning to the south and still\nsome sunshine. Some of the warmer spots will likely see highs \naround or over 80 degrees.\n\nCoverage should increase on Thursday. An upper level low will slowly \napproach from the Pacific Northwest. At the same time, a strong \nridge over the Southeast will begin to retrograde to the west \nand direct deeper moisture into the area, as much as 175 percent\nof normal. At the same time, a weak shortwave will be pivoting \naround the upper level low giving a bit more lift. However, \nthere is a limiting factor. It is that with the moisture in the \natmosphere, much of the day may end up being fairly cloudy. This\nmay limit instability somewhat, especially since it is now mid \nSeptember and not July. Nevertheless, there will be an \nincreased chance of showers, especially across western Wyoming. \nWith the increased cloud cover, temperatures will also be \ncooler, generally by about 5 to 10 degrees compared to \nWednesday.\n\nThe upper level low will begin to make a closer approach by Friday. \nOf all the days in the forecast, this looks like the most \nactive day across the area with almost all areas having at least\na 1 in 3 chance of convection during Friday and Friday evening.\nI have one more concern about this day as well. Most guidance \nis showing a 20 percent drop in precipitable water. However, \nthis could lead to a fairly sunny morning that could bring more \ninstability and a greater chance of convection, especially east\nof the Divide. If there could be local flooding, this could the\nmost likely day. The one mitigating factor continues to be the\nsteering flow, as much as 40 knots at times, so the storms will\nbe moving. And with more sun, temperatures will be warmer, but \nlikely not as warm as today.\n\nThe low will pass close to the area on Saturday, turning flow \nmore to the northwest. This should result in a noticable cooler \nday across the area. There will still be enough wrap around \nmoisture for some showers and isolated thunderstorms to \ncontinue. However, most guidance shows precipitable water values\nmuch lower on this day, so the threat of heavy rain looks less.\nUncertainty then increases from Sunday into early next week, as\na lot depends on the movement of the upper low. Some guidance \nmoves it far enough east for drier weather to move in for \nSunday, other guidance keeps showers around through Sunday \nnight. Confidence is not high in specifics as the models have \nhad different solutions in regards to speed and track of the \nupper level low for the past few days. However, we do have high \nconfidence of unsettled weather through at least Saturday. \nSomewhat drier and warmer weather may then return for early next\nweek.",
